WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.360
Hi. I'm Max.

2
00:00:01.360 --> 00:00:03.120
I'm a research engineer at Tessl

3
00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:06.760
where I work on building a platform for evaluating coding agents.

4
00:00:07.160 --> 00:00:10.840
What it means in practice is that we want to measure in a reliable way

5
00:00:11.080 --> 00:00:14.920
how agent performs in real code bases, why it fails,

6
00:00:15.080 --> 00:00:18.440
where it fails, and what can we do in order to fix the problems?

7
00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:23.720
As agents become a part of the everyday developer life cycle, knowing

8
00:00:23.720 --> 00:00:28.320
how to evaluate agents in your code base becomes a core skill for any engineer.

9
00:00:28.640 --> 00:00:33.080
This March, at AI Native Dev Meetup in London, we'll talk about

10
00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:36.360
how to build such platforms from scratch, how

11
00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:39.640
they work and what are the real challenges in designing them.

12
00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:43.320
Join us for a practical session where we will break down the key

13
00:00:43.320 --> 00:00:47.440
components of building an evaluation platform, and also explore how we build

14
00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:51.880
such a universal platform here at Tessl, where we allow you to evaluate

15
00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:56.920
any agent with any code base, with any context in your environment.
